+\subsection{Calibration}
+\label{sec:calibration}
+
+Single-pass decision models are used through their probabilities, e.g., a decision is automated when its probability
+exceeds a threshold and escalated otherwise, so how well these probabilities are calibrated matters as much as
+accuracy. \Cref{tab:calibration} reports the expected calibration error (ECE) of the decisions. Overall, \tf{} gives
+the best-calibrated models in every setting of the table, and the gap is largest on BIG-Bench Hard, which none of the
+models is trained on: after Knights-and-Knaves training, the ECE of answer-only models is 31.7 (12 epochs) and 38.7
+(20 epochs) against 7.8 for \tf{}. Longer answer-only training makes it worse on GSM8K as well (17.3 after 4 epochs,
+23.4 after 12). The placebo comes close to \tf{} in domain on GSM8K with the 4B model (15.0 vs.\ 14.4), but not on
+BIG-Bench Hard (17.4 vs.\ 11.8) and not with the 12B model (19.9 vs.\ 16.0), so better calibration is not merely a
+side effect of asking more questions. The largest difference is with a quarter of the arithmetic-chain data at 12B,
+where \tf{} has an ECE of 7.9 in range against 23.1 for answer-only training and 24.8 for the placebo.
